  "step_3": "The decision reason is based on skewness — specifically, preferring the lottery with a greater probability mass on the favorable side (i.e., more left-skewed). In this case, Lottery B has a smaller probability of loss (0.1% vs 0.2%), meaning its loss tail is thinner, so it is more left-skewed. Therefore, the reason would prefer B. However, the verbal report does not mention skewness, distribution shape, or the comparison of the probability mass on the favorable side. Instead, the individual incorrectly states that the loss probability is 0.2% and that they take a higher risk to lose less — which is not true in this case. The individual confuses the loss probability and the loss amount. There is no reference to the shape of the distribution or the skewness of the outcomes. The justification is based on a misunderstanding of the risk and loss structure. Therefore, the individual did not use the decision reason involving skewness. The reasoning is fundamentally different and based on a mischaracterization of the lotteries. Thus, the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
